    <item>
      <title>Demystifying the Dark Web Part 1: The Invisible Iceberg</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part1/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 04 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part1/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;🛑 Subject Briefing&#xA;    &lt;div id=&#34;-subject-briefing&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;    &lt;span&#xA;        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none&#34;&gt;&#xA;        &lt;a class=&#34;text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#-subject-briefing&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;In the realm of digital intelligence, operating without a map of the network’s hidden layers is a liability, not an option&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;This series deconstructs the architecture of the Dark Web, stripping away the sensationalism to examine the mechanical reality of how these shadow networks actually function. To secure a system, one must first understand the terrain where the most sophisticated threats reside.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
    <item>
      <title>Demystifying the Dark Web Part 2: The Onion Handshake</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-2/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 04 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-2/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;🛑 Dark Web as Offensive Infrastructure:&#xA;    &lt;div id=&#34;-dark-web-as-offensive-infrastructure&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;    &lt;span&#xA;        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none&#34;&gt;&#xA;        &lt;a class=&#34;text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#-dark-web-as-offensive-infrastructure&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;In the world of offensive security and research, infrastructure is the primary target. A Command &amp;amp; Control (C2) server or a research backend tied to a static IP is a liability. By routing through the Tor network, we decouple a service&amp;rsquo;s identity from its physical location. This ensures that even under heavy scrutiny, the backend remains a moving target, making traditional IP-based takedowns mathematically improbable.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
    <item>
      <title>Demystifying the Dark Web Part 3: Cryptographic Identity</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 04 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;🛠️ The Brute Force Setup: Mining Identity&#xA;    &lt;div id=&#34;-the-brute-force-setup-mining-identity&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;    &lt;span&#xA;        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none&#34;&gt;&#xA;        &lt;a class=&#34;text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#-the-brute-force-setup-mining-identity&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img&#xA;    class=&#34;my-0 rounded-md&#34;&#xA;    loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    decoding=&#34;async&#34;&#xA;    fetchpriority=&#34;auto&#34;&#xA;    alt=&#34;Brute Force Generation Speed&#34;&#xA;    width=&#34;876&#34;&#xA;    height=&#34;285&#34;&#xA;    src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/feature_hu_a17f81885ba64239.png&#34;&#xA;    srcset=&#34;http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/feature_hu_a17f81885ba64239.png 800w, http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/feature.png 1280w&#34;&#xA;    sizes=&#34;(min-width: 768px) 50vw, 65vw&#34;&#xA;    data-zoom-src=&#34;http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/feature.png&#34;&gt;&lt;figcaption&gt;mkp224o calculation results&lt;/figcaption&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;strong&gt;Unlike the clearnet, where you &amp;ldquo;rent&amp;rdquo; a domain from a central registrar like GoDaddy or Namecheap, an onion address is mined. It is a cryptographic discovery. To own an address that starts with a specific string, you have to iterate through billions of random key pairs until the math aligns. It is a raw expenditure of CPU cycles to prove ownership.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      <media:content xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-3/feature.png" />
    </item>
    
    <item>
      <title>Demystifying the Dark Web Part 4: The Operator&#39;s Grave</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-4/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 04 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/dark-web-part-4/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;🛑 [YOUR TURN: The Cold Open]&#xA;    &lt;div id=&#34;-your-turn-the-cold-open&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;    &lt;span&#xA;        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none&#34;&gt;&#xA;        &lt;a class=&#34;text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#-your-turn-the-cold-open&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Write 2-3 sentences here.&lt;/strong&gt;&#xA;&lt;em&gt;Explain that the math of Tor has never been &amp;lsquo;broken&amp;rsquo; in the wild. People aren&amp;rsquo;t caught because the encryption failed; they are caught because they were human. Mention &amp;lsquo;Digital Fingerprints&amp;rsquo;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;hr&gt;&#xA;&#xA;&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;The Traffic Correlation Trap&#xA;    &lt;div id=&#34;the-traffic-correlation-trap&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;    &lt;span&#xA;        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none&#34;&gt;&#xA;        &lt;a class=&#34;text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#the-traffic-correlation-trap&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Tor hides your IP, but it cannot hide &lt;strong&gt;Time&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
    <item>
      <title>Building a Manual PE Mapper in C&#43;&#43;</title>
      <link>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/my-first-malware/</link>
      <pubDate>Sun, 03 May 2026 18:00:00 +0100</pubDate>
      
      <guid>http://localhost:1313/operator-blog/posts/my-first-malware/</guid>
      <description>&lt;h2 class=&#34;relative group&#34;&gt;The Windows PE format&#xA;    &lt;div id=&#34;the-windows-pe-format&#34; class=&#34;anchor&#34;&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;    &lt;span&#xA;        class=&#34;absolute top-0 w-6 transition-opacity opacity-0 -start-6 not-prose group-hover:opacity-100 select-none&#34;&gt;&#xA;        &lt;a class=&#34;text-primary-300 dark:text-neutral-700 !no-underline&#34; href=&#34;#the-windows-pe-format&#34; aria-label=&#34;Anchor&#34;&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&#xA;    &lt;/span&gt;&#xA;    &#xA;&lt;/h2&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Before we map the excutable, we must understand the DOS Header&amp;hellip;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;I used &lt;code&gt;x64dgb&lt;/code&gt; to trace the &lt;code&gt;LoadLibraryA&lt;/code&gt; function.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Here is how i resolved the IAT :&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight-wrapper&#34;&gt;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; style=&#34;color:#f8f8f2;background-color:#272822;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-cpp&#34; data-lang=&#34;cpp&#34;&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;#include&amp;lt;windows.h&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#75715e&#34;&gt;#include&amp;lt;iostream&amp;gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#a6e22e&#34;&gt;main&lt;/span&gt;(){&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    std&lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;::&lt;/span&gt;cout &lt;span style=&#34;color:#f92672&#34;&gt;&amp;lt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#e6db74&#34;&gt;&amp;#34;Parsing NT Headers...&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;    &lt;span style=&#34;color:#66d9ef&#34;&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&#34;color:#ae81ff&#34;&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&#34;display:flex;&#34;&gt;&lt;span&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;figure&gt;&lt;img&#xA;    class=&#34;my-0 rounded-md&#34;&#xA;    loading=&#34;lazy&#34;&#xA;    decoding=&#34;async&#34;&#xA;    fetchpriority=&#34;low&#34;&#xA;    alt=&#34;The IT guy&#34;&#xA;    src=&#34;images/IT_guy.png&#34;&#xA;    &gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;</description>
      
    </item>
    
